==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɛnd/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-lend.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɪnd/|a=US,pin-pen}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlend/|a=AU,NZ}}
** {{IPA|en|[ˈlend]|~|[ˈle̞nd]|a=AU}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈle̝nd/|~|[ˈlɪnd]|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛnd|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|lind|aa=pin-pen}}

===Etymology 1===
From earlier {{m|en|len}} (with excrescent ''-d'', as in {{l|en|sound|soun''d''}}), from {{inh|en|enm|lenen}}, {{m|enm|lænen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|lǣnan||to lend; give, grant, lease}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*laihnijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*laihnijaną||to loan}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*laihną||loan}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*leykʷ-||to leave, leave over}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|len}}, {{m|sco|lend||to lend}}, {{cog|fy|liene||to lend, borrow, loan}}, {{cog|nl|lenen||to lend, borrow, loan}}, {{cog|da|låne||to lend, loan}}, {{cog|sv|låna||to lend, loan}}, {{cog|is|lána||to lend, loan}}, {{cog|is|léna||to grant}}, {{cog|la|linquō||quit, leave, forlet}}, {{cog|grc|λείπω||leave, release}}. See also {{l|en|loan}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|lent,lended<q:nonstandard>}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[allow]] to be used by someone [[temporarily]], on [[condition]] that it or its [[equivalent]] will be [[#Verb|return]]ed.
#: {{ux|en|I will only '''lend''' you my car if you fill up the tank.}}
#: {{ux|en|Where is that hundred euros I '''lent''' you months ago?}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=1 June 2013|title=End of the peer show|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21578670-peer-peer-lending-needs-new-name-end-peer-show|volume=407|issue=8838|page=71|passage=Finance is seldom romantic. But the idea of peer-to-peer lending comes close. This is an industry that brings together individual savers and lenders on online platforms. Those that want to borrow are matched with those that want to '''lend'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180103143947/https://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21578670-peer-peer-lending-needs-new-name-end-peer-show|archivedate=3 January 2018}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make a [[loan]].
# {{lb|en|reflexive}} To be [[suitable]] or [[applicable]], to [[fit]].
#: {{ux|en|Poems do not '''lend''' themselves to translation easily.}}
#: {{ux|en|The long history of the past does not '''lend''' itself to a simple black and white interpretation.}}
# To afford; to grant or furnish in general.
#: {{ux|en|Can you '''lend''' me some assistance?}}
#: {{ux|en|The famous director '''lent''' his name to the new film.}}
#: {{ux|en|The old silver '''lends''' an air of elegance to an otherwise drab room.}}
#: {{ux|en|The evidence '''lends''' credence to the witness's testimony.}}
#: {{ux|en|The subtle use of strings '''lends'''  fluidity to the composition.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Cato|page=23|passage={{...}} ''Cato'', '''lend''' me for a vvhile thy Patience, / And condeſcend to hear a young Man ſpeak.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|author=w:John Addington Symonds|title=Sir Philip Sidney
|passage=Mountain lines and distant horizons '''lend''' space and largeness to his compositions.}}
# {{lb|en|proscribed}} To [[borrow]].

