===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*lendʰ- (loin)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|lende}} (usually in plural as {{m|enm|lendes}}, {{m|enm|leendes}}, {{m|enm|lyndes}}), from {{inh|en|ang|lendenu}}, {{m|ang|lendino||loins}}, plural of {{der|en|ang|lenden|t=loin}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*landī}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*landį̄|t=loin}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*lendʰ-||loin, kidney}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|lend}}, {{m|sco|leynd||the loins, flank, buttocks}}, {{cog|nl|lendenen||loins, reins}}, {{cog|de|Lenden||loins}}, {{cog|sv|länder||loins}}, {{cog|da|lænd||loin}}, {{cog|is|lendar||loins}}, {{cog|la|lumbus||loin}} (whence {{m|en|loin}}),
{{cog|pl|lędźwie||loins}},
{{cog|ru|ля́двея||thigh, haunch}}. {{etydate|{{w|Old English}} period}}, and {{lg|obsolete}} outside dialects after 1568.

